itle":"IMG_5108","uploaded":"2016-06-30 12:54:07","width":3999},"27927121331":{"albums":[{"id":"72157643911711523","position":6092}],"bytes":2342452,"camera_position":6092,"comments":[],"description":"","detail":"photos/0103.json","filename":"27927121331_2dd66800e7_o.jpg","flickr_url":"https://www.flickr.com/photos/earthwandering/27927121331/","height":2042,"id":"27927121331","kind":"photo","license":"All Rights Reserved","original":"originals/27927121331/27927121331_2dd66800e7_o.jpg","sha256":"c756f624b180ecdd292cff46a64d9925c5cd5adb453abf137f051c4a5393cca1","tags":[],"taken":"2016-05-21 16:44:28","title":"IMG_5081","uploaded":"2016-06-30 12:54:35","width":3525},"27953107920":{"albums":[{"id":"72157643911711523","position":6188}],"bytes":3368660,"camera_position":6188,"comments":[{"author":"94951113@N04","date":"2016-07-11 02:47:39","text":"Takeout for squirrels?!  :-)"}],"description":"","detail":"photos/0103.json","filename":"27953107920_1f00d55779_o.jpg","flickr_url":"https://www.flickr.com/photos/earthwandering/27953107920/","height":2739,"id":"27953107920","kind":"photo","license":"All Rights Reserved","original":"originals/27953107920/27953107920_1f00d55779_o.jpg","sha256":"5a04a677164e3c6505285266b860f09a3649f3af4350566547c486980bbdd7e7","tags":["oregon","portland"],"taken":"2016-06-11 14:14:14","title":"IMG_5788","uploaded":"2016-07-10 21:56:34","width":3458},"27959021715":{"albums":[{"id":"72157643908329683","position":211},{"id":"72157643911711523","position":6081},{"id":"72157687398999470","position":64}],"bytes":3616606,"camera_position":6081,"comments":[{"author":"26941526@N00","date":"2016-06-30 14:35:58","text":"What to expect..."}],"description":"Port Townsend Rhody Parade","detail":"photos/0103.json","filename":"27959021715_7ff9c87789_o.jpg","flickr_url":"https://www.flickr.com/photos/earthwandering/27959021715/","height":2584,"id":"27959021715","kind":"photo","license":"All Rights Reserved","original":"originals/27959021715/27959021715_7ff9c87789_o.jpg","sha256":"e1eeb432d7df82a90971b0fb97c1ba6cf004ccf097e061a093e73357b3c7f075","tags":["port townsend","washington","rhody parade"],"taken":"2016-05-21 13:28:01","title":"IMG_4894","uploaded":"2016-06-28 06:46:11","width":3850},"27962041064":{"albums":[{"id":"72157643911711523","position":6336},{"id":"72157671912177895","position":75}],"bytes":2942145,"camera_position":6336,"comments":[{"author":"66325452@N02","date":"2016-07-27 13:03:28","text":"For safety's sake when on the water it is almost always best to drink responsibly, but wearing a helmet might be going a bit overboard. Safe to say, wonderful photograph!"},{"author":"100330886@N04","date":"2016-07-29 06:42:53","text":"We seem to switch gear (ha!) frequently and often forget some piece for the next mode of travel.  Helmets are both good sun hats and flotation.  :)  Thanks!  Your recent pics of flying and rafting were very cool!  Are comments disabled intentionally?"},{"author":"66325452@N02","date":"2016-07-29 08:42:01","text":"I started out on Flickr pretty early on with a different screen name and at that time I allowed comments. My writing style is as John Muir described his, slow as a glacier. And although I love writing, I found I was spending more time than I wanted responding to comments, less time doing other things that I like. My early account was more aimed at mountaineering and kayaking, and along with that came questions about routes and other outdoor details that I felt responsible to answer correctly, sometimes for safety's sake. Thoughtful concise answers, but behind the scenes, me moving slow as a glacier. Sometimes the answer to the person's quick question was just a Google click away, and at times I never knew if the question was genuine or not, the person maybe just wanting to be Flickr friendly. I also wanted to distance myself from the chain of people building social status, for lack of a better word.\n\nThose that do leave comments are able to because I have added a photograph into a group that they are also members. On those photos I have no control over comments, and I do enjoy reading those comments when they are left. I also like responding via Flickr email, when a thoughtful question or comment comes from that direction.  My main interest in Flickr beyond the immediate satisfaction of wonderful people like you faving one of my photos is knowing the power of search is always at work behind the scenes, and some individual that I may never know stumbled onto a photo that was exactly what they were looking for, or were totally surprised in what they just found. That is why I tag photos with key words and include a rudimentary description; I'm always fishing for people that are on the search. For me adding photos to Flickr groups is just another step in that direction and is worthwhile.\n\nProbably more than you wanted to know, feel fortunate I gave the highly abbreviated answer that only touched on much deeper issues within me. Questions always leave my head spinning with all their connections. Apart from that, I hope you understand that my occasional comments are almost always left with a touch of intended humor on my part. I have loved following your photostream, the whole damn story as it unfolds into a life well lived.  I've never been much for sitting on my butt as life flies by; photostreams such as yours gives me inspiration to keep moving, keep doing, keep living life... and work to pass that zest onto others when and while I can."},{"author":"100330886@N04","date":"2020-01-07 23:46:52","text":"[https://www.flickr.com/photos/tellytomtelly] Well I'm glad you commented here in great detail.  And here's me replying at a glaciers pace.  :)  Hope all is well!"}],"description":"","detail":"photos/0103.json","filename":"27962041064_8be10faa7e_o.jpg","flickr_url":"https://www.flickr.com/photos/earthwandering/27962041064/","height":2756,"id":"27962041064","kind":"photo","license":"All Rights Reserved","original":"originals/27962041064/279620